WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.060 --> 00:00:06.759
Hello everyone, welcome to another NCBI Minute.

00:00:06.759 --> 00:00:10.620
Today I will talk about the advanced search
in dbGaP.

00:00:10.620 --> 00:00:14.660
This first slide is just a reminder about
a few housekeeping details.

00:00:14.660 --> 00:00:23.590
We will be recording and there is a link to
the YouTube channel.

00:00:23.590 --> 00:00:30.919
Today we will talk about an advanced interface
to dbGaP, I assume if you are attending the

00:00:30.919 --> 00:00:33.010
webinar you know a lot about it already.

00:00:33.010 --> 00:00:37.730
I just want to introduce a little bit of information
about dbGaP, and then I'm going to show you

00:00:37.730 --> 00:00:42.660
a couple of slides that show you the interfaces
I'm going to talk about, then we will go live

00:00:42.660 --> 00:00:44.960
on a demonstration on how to do this.

00:00:44.960 --> 00:00:49.810
So dbGaP stands for the database of genotypes
and phenotypes.

00:00:49.810 --> 00:00:54.950
It contains human phenotype data and related
individual level molecular data.

00:00:54.950 --> 00:01:04.500
There's a lot of studies in dbGaP encompassing
lots of different numbers of participants,

00:01:04.500 --> 00:01:06.729
from a few to tens of thousands.

00:01:06.729 --> 00:01:15.380
There are about 1 million people represented
in dbGaP, across 600 studies, it contains

00:01:15.380 --> 00:01:26.280
information about the studies, molecular data,
medical images, research protocols, and questionaires.

00:01:26.280 --> 00:01:32.509
Molecular data types include genotypes, expression
level, genomic sequence, epigenomic information,

00:01:32.509 --> 00:01:39.060
somatic mutation, and human microbiome information.

00:01:39.060 --> 00:01:47.850
In order to get that data you have to have
controlled access approval.

00:01:47.850 --> 00:01:56.659
Here is a link to the homepage that talks
about how to go about doing that.

00:01:56.659 --> 00:02:00.400
From the web point of view, the main way into
dbGaP is through the dbGaP homepage, which

00:02:00.400 --> 00:02:07.609
is accessible from our homepage or from Google
or any search engine, you will just type in

00:02:07.609 --> 00:02:10.149
dbGaP and get to this page.

00:02:10.149 --> 00:02:13.830
The main search box on this page is basically
an Entrez search interface.

00:02:13.830 --> 00:02:18.760
And you are probably familiar with those for
our molecular databases, and in fact PubMed

00:02:18.760 --> 00:02:20.489
is an Entrez interface.

00:02:20.489 --> 00:02:24.680
What we have available now on dbGaP is a separate
Advanced search page.

00:02:24.680 --> 00:02:28.959
It makes it a little easier to find the data
you need; it's a better adapted interface

00:02:28.959 --> 00:02:31.280
working with dbGaP data.

00:02:31.280 --> 00:02:35.950
Click on the link shown on the home page,
you will go to that interface.

00:02:35.950 --> 00:02:42.319
It is an alternative way to search dbGaP on
the web, finding studies and other things

00:02:42.319 --> 00:02:44.200
you need.

00:02:44.200 --> 00:02:49.409
Basically this is what you will call a faceted
search, you will filter the studies on the

00:02:49.409 --> 00:02:56.959
right, that let you filter by variables, the
phenotype data sets, molecular datasets analyses,

00:02:56.959 --> 00:03:03.379
study, keyword search, study design, molecular
data type, and marker sets.

00:03:03.379 --> 00:03:08.620
You can do a keyword search across all of
these aspects of data.

00:03:08.620 --> 00:03:13.460
While you are doing this and searching the
query, there is a sort of SQL query that is

00:03:13.460 --> 00:03:18.440
formed, on the top you can adjust those; click
on them to remove them.

00:03:18.440 --> 00:03:21.260
There is a link [www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/projects/gapsolr/facets.html]
that goes directly to this page.

00:03:21.260 --> 00:03:41.230
What I want to do now is leave the slides
and take you to this faceted interface and

00:03:41.230 --> 00:03:44.610
do a couple of simple examples to show you
how it works.

00:03:44.610 --> 00:03:52.849
I'm going to escape out of PowerPoint and
switch to the web browser, set on the NCBI

00:03:52.849 --> 00:04:04.230
homepage, if you want to follow along you
certainly can launch a web browser.

00:04:04.230 --> 00:04:09.099
The quickest way to get to the dbGaP homepage,
one way to do this anyway, is to use the pulldown

00:04:09.099 --> 00:04:14.849
list, select dbGaP, and click Search.

00:04:14.849 --> 00:04:16.260
Here is the dbGaP homepage.

00:04:16.260 --> 00:04:19.100
This is where a lot of documentation is.

00:04:19.100 --> 00:04:24.740
The information about controlled access data
is here, if you want to learn more about that,

00:04:24.740 --> 00:04:27.330
or anything you need to know about dbGaP is
available here.

00:04:27.330 --> 00:04:33.220
I'm going to click on this Advanced Search
interface; it will take me to the new search

00:04:33.220 --> 00:04:35.280
interface.

00:04:35.280 --> 00:04:41.569
there are several tabs here, variables, phenotype
data sets, documents, molecular data sets,

00:04:41.569 --> 00:04:45.970
analysis, and the number of all these things
available for searching.

00:04:45.970 --> 00:04:55.240
Over here I have a set of filters, with the
numbers of things that each one of these aspects

00:04:55.240 --> 00:04:56.370
I can filter by.

00:04:56.370 --> 00:05:00.600
We will use these to get a particular kind
of data out of here.

00:05:00.600 --> 00:05:07.770
Notice the filters on the left hand side change
whenever, for example if I click on this variables

00:05:07.770 --> 00:05:15.090
tab, for instance, there is now a new filter
to filter by, Study, if I want to do that.

00:05:15.090 --> 00:05:18.150
Likewise there are different filters further
down.

00:05:18.150 --> 00:05:21.880
They pertain to that particular kind of data.

00:05:21.880 --> 00:05:29.450
For example if I go over here and click on
Analyses, the number of studies changes dramatically.

00:05:29.450 --> 00:05:33.259
There are 76 studies that have analyses available.

00:05:33.259 --> 00:05:38.949
We will see that happen when we do our searches
today, when we go through our example.

00:05:38.949 --> 00:05:42.919
I will start an example with the Studies tab
selected.

00:05:42.919 --> 00:05:50.140
I will start out as I am interested in a particular
human disease, I will pick a disease focus.

00:05:50.140 --> 00:05:53.330
I want to look up Type II diabetes.

00:05:53.330 --> 00:05:57.330
I can see what is available by typing diabetes.

00:05:57.330 --> 00:06:06.710
And i'm going to click the checkbox here where
you see eleven type II diabetes studies.

00:06:06.710 --> 00:06:11.590
And then there might be other criteria I might
want to narrow this down by.

00:06:11.590 --> 00:06:18.229
One thing you might want to do is to pay attention
to what kind of consent is required.

00:06:18.229 --> 00:06:21.250
This varies a lot depending upon the particular
study.

00:06:21.250 --> 00:06:24.590
In some cases you are restricted to doing
research on a particular topic.

00:06:24.590 --> 00:06:28.500
The most general is General Research Use,
shown here.

00:06:28.500 --> 00:06:34.610
I will pick a restricted one, General Research
Use plus Institutional Research Board approval.

00:06:34.610 --> 00:06:37.090
I will check that one.

00:06:37.090 --> 00:06:41.849
And so I'm left with a small set of two studies.

00:06:41.849 --> 00:06:47.020
I'm sorry there are three studies.

00:06:47.020 --> 00:06:49.639
One of them is a parent of the other two.

00:06:49.639 --> 00:06:54.830
This is a fusion study, the Finland-United
States Investigation of NIDDM genetics study.

00:06:54.830 --> 00:06:57.819
I can pick one of these if I want to learn
more about it.

00:06:57.819 --> 00:07:02.509
If I am interested in sequence data, and notice
that I can narrow this down by what kind of

00:07:02.509 --> 00:07:08.449
data available, so I could pick, for example,
whole exome if I wanted to.

00:07:08.449 --> 00:07:12.310
I'm gonna go ahead and look at this study.

00:07:12.310 --> 00:07:21.580
If I click on the main link there, it takes
me into the dbGaP information about the study.

00:07:21.580 --> 00:07:27.759
I can see it is a sub study of this larger
one, and to get authorized access I'd have

00:07:27.759 --> 00:07:30.860
to look at the larger study to see what is
required.

00:07:30.860 --> 00:07:36.569
There is public information available on the
ftp site, the rest is controlled access.

00:07:36.569 --> 00:07:38.800
I can read about what it is.

00:07:38.800 --> 00:07:45.860
This is looking at people that have insulin
insensitivity, basically they had cases and

00:07:45.860 --> 00:07:46.919
controls.

00:07:46.919 --> 00:07:54.789
Whether they are case or control is based
on glucose tolerance results and fasting glucose

00:07:54.789 --> 00:07:55.789
levels.

00:07:55.789 --> 00:08:01.360
We can go back to take a look at the distribution
of those glucose levels, if we want to, by

00:08:01.360 --> 00:08:04.830
using facets to find that particular variable.

00:08:04.830 --> 00:08:07.639
Let me go back.

00:08:07.639 --> 00:08:14.880
I will add a keyword to this.

00:08:14.880 --> 00:08:21.030
I will add the keyword glucose.

00:08:21.030 --> 00:08:24.729
Notice that as I construct my query, and I
didn't point this out before, I have these

00:08:24.729 --> 00:08:31.170
little lozenges up here, these graphics to
show me how the query is constructed.

00:08:31.170 --> 00:08:36.579
Now notice that I'm really interested in variables
that are associated with this.

00:08:36.579 --> 00:08:43.190
What I want to do is click that tab and what
I can see is, I have fasting plasma glucose

00:08:43.190 --> 00:08:44.380
available.

00:08:44.380 --> 00:08:47.120
And a 2 hour glucose from my tolerance test
here.

00:08:47.120 --> 00:08:52.470
So let me go ahead and click on the Variable
page for fasting plasma glucose.

00:08:52.470 --> 00:09:02.180
So here I have a nice graph that shows me
what the glucose levels are for the people

00:09:02.180 --> 00:09:07.410
in the case group; people that are potentially
diabetic; and in the control group.

00:09:07.410 --> 00:09:10.959
And here is the overall distribution of glucose
levels here.

00:09:10.959 --> 00:09:14.970
That's a quick way to find out that information.

00:09:14.970 --> 00:09:20.540
I am going back to the facets page.

00:09:20.540 --> 00:09:26.690
Had I visited that page by opening a new link,
my previous search that I had would be here

00:09:26.690 --> 00:09:28.580
and I'd hve to clear it to get results.

00:09:28.580 --> 00:09:31.960
What I am interested in now is a different
kind of variable.

00:09:31.960 --> 00:09:36.420
I'm going to be looking at something called
bilirubin, which is a breakdown product of

00:09:36.420 --> 00:09:40.180
hemoglobin that's in peoples bloodstream and
excreted in the bile.

00:09:40.180 --> 00:09:44.630
Some people are walking with fairly high levels
of bilirubin in their bloodstream.

00:09:44.630 --> 00:09:50.709
I will look for analyses that are about Bilirubin.

00:09:50.709 --> 00:09:58.590
I will select that, and I get 8, they are
from two studies.

00:09:58.590 --> 00:10:04.090
One of them is the Framingham cohort study,
which is that longitudinal study that many

00:10:04.090 --> 00:10:05.090
of you are familiar with.

00:10:05.090 --> 00:10:11.820
And there is another one which is pretty interesting,
the National Institute on Aging Sardinia study.

00:10:11.820 --> 00:10:18.171
This is looking at a population of people
living on the island of Sardinia; it's kind

00:10:18.171 --> 00:10:19.830
of a population genetics study.

00:10:19.830 --> 00:10:22.360
let me select that one.

00:10:22.360 --> 00:10:33.639
Now I have two kinds of Bilirubin, fractionaled
and total Bilirubin, these will be genome

00:10:33.639 --> 00:10:36.510
wide association results.

00:10:36.510 --> 00:10:40.520
This gives me a convenient link to the Genome
Browser, which I'm going to show you in a

00:10:40.520 --> 00:10:41.520
minute.

00:10:41.520 --> 00:10:46.050
There is also a link here for the PheGeni,
the Phenotype Genotype Integrator, another

00:10:46.050 --> 00:10:52.580
useful tool to browse and search for GWAS
results.

00:10:52.580 --> 00:11:00.150
I will save time, I have loaded the Genome
browser, sometimes it takes a while to load,

00:11:00.150 --> 00:11:03.560
I will show you what that looks like.

00:11:03.560 --> 00:11:08.120
Basically gives me, if I click the link you
could see this.

00:11:08.120 --> 00:11:14.420
This gives me the P values, basically, SNPs
that are in a particular position on the chromosome

00:11:14.420 --> 00:11:18.959
and their association, in this case with Bilirubin.

00:11:18.959 --> 00:11:27.350
If I click here I will load the browser that
shows me the P values, so those association

00:11:27.350 --> 00:11:29.230
results here.

00:11:29.230 --> 00:11:36.770
There is a cluster of them right here; in
a nice little linkage group between these

00:11:36.770 --> 00:11:39.520
two recombination rate peak here.

00:11:39.520 --> 00:11:44.769
They all map to a particular gene, a complex
locus.

00:11:44.769 --> 00:11:51.290
This is one of the glucuronosyltransferase
genes that is present on chromosome 2.

00:11:51.290 --> 00:11:56.050
This is actually a complex locus with a bunch
of different products.

00:11:56.050 --> 00:12:01.860
One I'm interested in, I want to show you
down here is UGT1A1.

00:12:01.860 --> 00:12:06.620
I can click over to the Gene database.

00:12:06.620 --> 00:12:16.790
This is the UGT1A1 polypeptide, it is associated
with some conditions with high bilirubin,

00:12:16.790 --> 00:12:22.140
one of them is called Gilbert syndrome, which
is a very common condition; in fact I actually

00:12:22.140 --> 00:12:23.340
have that condition.

00:12:23.340 --> 00:12:30.339
This is how to use the faceted interface to
find the different types of data in dbGaP.

00:12:30.339 --> 00:12:35.589
Let me switch back to the slides for a minute,
just to show you a couple more resources that

00:12:35.589 --> 00:12:38.709
may be useful.

00:12:38.709 --> 00:12:41.339
Some links to some things.

00:12:41.339 --> 00:12:45.420
So some additional tools, one I mentioned
today was the Phenotype Genotype Integrator.

00:12:45.420 --> 00:12:50.820
This is a good way to look at association
results, like the ones we saw at the end.

00:12:50.820 --> 00:12:53.540
There is also the dbGaP data browser.

00:12:53.540 --> 00:12:58.810
That particular tool is relatively new and
requires authorized access.

00:12:58.810 --> 00:13:04.610
For documentation, much of the dbGaP help
is linked to the homepage.

00:13:04.610 --> 00:13:07.050
Here are some direct links to it.

00:13:07.050 --> 00:13:09.980
Tao has created a bunch of factsheets for
NCBI.

00:13:09.980 --> 00:13:11.510
These are very useful.

00:13:11.510 --> 00:13:15.370
I have a direct link there to the one on dbGaP.

00:13:15.370 --> 00:13:18.050
A reminder that we have a YouTube channel.

00:13:18.050 --> 00:13:20.361
This video and a number of other ones are
available there.

00:13:20.361 --> 00:13:26.450
There is a link there to a README for all
of the factsheets.

